[0.005, "o", "event:final\r\ndata:{\"answer\":\"To make a GET request with HTTPX, you can use the top‑level helper or a `Client`. For example:\\n\\n```pycon\\n>>> import httpx\\n>>> r = httpx.get('https://httpbin.org/get')\\n>>> r\\n<Response [200 OK]>\\n```\\n\\nor with a client context:\\n\\n```pycon\\n>>> with httpx.Client() as client:\\n... r = client.get('https://example.com')\\n... print(r.status_code)\\n```\\n\\nBoth approaches send a GET request and return a `Response` object. [2][1]\",\"citations\":[{\"index\":1,\"title\":\"clients.md\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/encode/httpx/blob/master/docs/advanced/clients.md\",\"headingPath\":\"Making requests\",\"snippet\":\"Once you have a `Client`, you can send requests using `.get()`, `.post()`, etc. For example: ```pycon >>> with httpx.Client() as client: ... r = client.get('https://example.com') ... >>> r <Response [200 OK]> ``` These methods accept the same arguments as `httpx.get()`, `httpx.post()`, etc. This mea...\"},{\"index\":2,\"title\":\"quickstart.md\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/encode/httpx/blob/master/docs/quickstart.md\",\"headingPath\":\"QuickStart\",\"snippet\":\"First, start by importing HTTPX: ```pycon >>> import httpx ``` Now, let’s try to get a webpage. ```pycon >>> r = httpx.get('https://httpbin.org/get') >>> r <Response [200 OK]> ``` Similarly, to make an HTTP POST request: ```pycon >>> r = httpx.post('https://httpbin.org/post', data={'key': 'value'}) ...\"}],\"refused\":false,\"latencyMs\":548,\"cached\":false}\r\n\r\n"]
[1.008, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m== 6. MCP tools ==\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "Tools: list_repositories, search_docs, ask_repo, get_document\r\nHTTP:  /mcp  (streamable HTTP, requires X-API-Key)\r\nstdio: java -jar repolens-0.1.0.jar --spring.main.web-application-type=none \\\r\n         --spring.ai.mcp.server.stdio=true --logging.config=classpath:logback-stdio.xml\r\n       See docs/MCP_SETUP.md for the Claude Desktop config.\r\n"]
